    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1261">

                <pb facs="1229" n="943" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg943"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-03-23T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3080. In a telegram dated March 18 the American Ambassador Berlin expresses the
                    opinion that it would be most unfortunate if <pb facs="1230" n="944"
                        xml:id="pg944"/> the German authorities had to cease caring for wounded
                    prisoners in order to save gloves for their own people. He adds that rubber
                    gloves are used in work on civil and military prisoners, and that wounded
                    prisoners have been splendidly treated. He believes the matter to be urgent.
                    This is not a matter for formal communication to the British Foreign Office. You
                    may, however, at your discretion, informally notify such parties as you deem
                    proper of private opinion American Ambassador, Berlin.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
